#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f4dc7f6-d085-5ad8-8ac3-95e5b9cc337f" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "99376061-f11c-50f9-8c6e-e67ad640afd1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1331" :question/problem "Factor $9x^2+30x+25$." :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\na^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}a{\\color{blue}{b}} + {\\color{blue}b}^2 = (a+{\\color{blue}{b}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9x^2+30x+25\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n(3x)^{2} + 30x + (5)^{2}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(3x)^{2} + 2(15x) + (5)^{2}$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(15x)$ as a product of $3x$ and ${\\color{blue}5}$:\n$(3x)^{2} + 2(3x \\cdot 5) + (5)^{2}$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=3x$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{b}} = {\\color{blue}{5}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n$(3x + 5)^{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f64efad-73ef-5caa-b38b-060d24ce13cf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Whose Terms Have a Common Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "e9c8bdc9-696d-5a55-bb0d-d247008c5684" :question/math-academy-id "e-1333" :question/problem "Factor the expression $7a^2+28a+28$." :question/worked-solution "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $7$. Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $7$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7a^{2} + 28a + 28 &=  \\\\\n&= 7(a^{2} + 4a + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na^{2} + 4a + 4 &=  \\\\\n&= a^{2} + 4a + 2^{2} \\\\\n&= a^{2} + 2 \\cdot 2a + 2^{2} \\\\\n&= (a + 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7(a^{2} + 4a + 4) &=  \\\\\n&= 7(a + 2)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b67de190-3b32-506e-b375-bd18d4543bf6" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "b74ed282-c54c-56f8-aa9f-8c5ef4465712" :question/math-academy-id "e-1334" :question/problem "Factor $-16y^2 + 4y - \\dfrac{1}{4}$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-16y^{2} + 4y - \\frac{1}{4} &=  \\\\\n&= - (16y^{2} - 4y + \\frac{1}{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the sum of difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16y^{2} - 4y + \\frac{1}{4} &=  \\\\\n(4y)^{2} - 4y + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n(4y)^{2} - 2 \\cdot (2y) + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n(4y)^{2} - 2 \\cdot (4y \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) + (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (4y - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (16y^{2} - 4y + \\frac{1}{4}) &=  \\\\\n&= - (4y - \\frac{1}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068520 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068902 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69a3b7a7-1042-511a-aaff-8b0911eae499" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Generated by the Square of a Difference"}] :question/id #uuid "b7141e58-61cf-5134-8443-a82d79a5506e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1593" :question/problem "Factor $9y^2-12y+4$." :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\na^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}a{\\color{blue}{b}} + {\\color{blue}b}^2 = (a-{\\color{blue}{b}})^2\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9y^2-12y+4\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n$(3y)^{2} - 12y + (2)^{2}$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n$(3y)^{2} - 2(6y) + (2)^{2}$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(6y)$ as a product of $3y$ and ${\\color{blue}2}$:\n$(3y)^{2} - 2(3y \\cdot 2) + (2)^{2}$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=3y$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{b}} = {\\color{blue}{2}}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n$(3y - 2)^{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f64efad-73ef-5caa-b38b-060d24ce13cf" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Perfect Square Trinomial Whose Terms Have a Common Factor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7ff83ef-b4b8-5404-93d2-e3ff8c44e0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(5y^{2} + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066499} {:answer/id #uuid "deff837a-170b-54f6-a0a2-f1d9a4c3f5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y^{2} + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066500} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1834e0-2cf3-5037-bd92-9c5f0b5b4a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(5y + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066501} {:answer/id #uuid "786a1a8c-1a55-569c-8408-ccef1b46ec95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(y + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066502} {:answer/id #uuid "7c768211-6163-5b1e-94a3-8799021b6845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25(5y + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066503}] :answer-field/id #uuid "32d801e5-57bf-5096-8355-daa85cc37fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066498}] :question/id #uuid "11047f98-24ac-5e85-994b-d2cd73e1984b" :question/math-academy-id "q-3009" :question/problem "Factor $25y^{2} + 50y + 25$."}]}
